ow: sta channel override caps the AP's width at its configured width

opensync-rpi / opensync-lab: with an active backhaul STA, the override gives every AP
of the radio the STA's operational channel, its width included. On a 2.4 GHz pod AP
configured HT20 (the pod's Wifi_Radio_Config) behind a root AP on 40 MHz, the AP was
asked for 40 MHz (hostapd ht_capab=[HT40+], op_class 83); hostapd keeps it at 20 MHz
(20/40 coexistence on a busy band), so confsync kept requesting 2437 (40/2447), never
settled, and its watchdog aborted owm every ~186 s ("confsync didn't settle for
180 s"). Each owm restart made cm drop and rebuild the backhaul's GRE. Seen on both
Raspberry Pi pods on the physical MV3's 2.4 GHz onboarding BSS (channel 6, 40 MHz).

The AP now follows the STA's control channel no wider than it is configured (when it
is configured: the module's unit tests leave it unset and are unchanged), and the
divergence check compares control channels, as the AP may be narrower.

Upstream: an OpenSync core bug as released in 6.6.1.0:
ow_sta_channel_override_ap_mutate_vif() in src/lib/ow/src/ow_sta_channel_override.c
copies the STA link's channel onto the AP whole, width included, whatever the AP's
configured width (and ow_sta_channel_override_ap_update() treats a width difference
as divergence). Any extender whose AP is configured narrower than its root AP runs
hits it when its driver or hostapd cannot run the wider width. Not reported (the
owner decides).

diff --git a/src/lib/ow/src/ow_sta_channel_override.c b/src/lib/ow/src/ow_sta_channel_override.c
index 5b168350..bcc3124e 100644
--- a/src/lib/ow/src/ow_sta_channel_override.c
+++ b/src/lib/ow/src/ow_sta_channel_override.c
@@ -221,8 +221,12 @@ ow_sta_channel_override_ap_set_info__(struct ow_sta_channel_override_ap *ap,
         const struct osw_channel *sta_chan = ow_sta_channel_override_ap_get_sta_chan(ap);
         if (sta_chan == NULL) return;
 
+        /* The AP follows the STA's control channel; it may be
+         * narrower (no wider than it is configured, see
+         * ow_sta_channel_override_ap_mutate_vif()).
+         */
         const bool divergent_channels = (next == NULL)
-                                     || (memcmp(sta_chan, next, sizeof(*sta_chan)) != 0);
+                                     || (sta_chan->control_freq_mhz != next->control_freq_mhz);
         if (divergent_channels) {
             ow_sta_channel_override_ap_start_settling(ap);
         }
@@ -504,16 +508,29 @@ ow_sta_channel_override_ap_mutate_vif(struct ow_sta_channel_override_ap *ap,
     if (vif->vif_type != OSW_VIF_AP) return;
 
     const enum ow_sta_channel_override_action action = ow_sta_channel_override_ap_get_action(ap, vif);
-    const struct osw_channel *new_chan = ow_sta_channel_override_action_get_channel(ap, vif, action);
+    struct osw_channel new_chan = *ow_sta_channel_override_action_get_channel(ap, vif, action);
+
+    /* Follow the root AP's channel, but no wider than this AP
+     * is configured: a 2.4 GHz AP configured HT20 behind a 40
+     * MHz root AP was otherwise asked for 40 MHz, which its
+     * hostapd cannot keep (20/40 coexistence), so confsync
+     * never settled and its watchdog aborted owm every 3
+     * minutes, taking the backhaul's GRE down each time.
+     */
+    const bool ap_chan_configured = (vif->u.ap.channel.control_freq_mhz != 0);
+    if (action == OW_STA_CHANNEL_OVERRIDE_USE_STA_STATE_CHAN && ap_chan_configured) {
+        const enum osw_channel_width w = osw_channel_width_min(new_chan.width, vif->u.ap.channel.width);
+        osw_channel_downgrade_to(&new_chan, w);
+    }
 
     ow_sta_channel_override_ap_mutate_ap_log(ap,
                                              ap->last_action,
                                              action,
                                              &ap->last_channel,
                                              &vif->u.ap.channel,
-                                             new_chan);
+                                             &new_chan);
 
-    vif->u.ap.channel = *new_chan;
+    vif->u.ap.channel = new_chan;
     ap->last_action = action;
     ap->last_channel = vif->u.ap.channel;
 }
